@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Simple BPMN collaboration — 2 pools with 1-2 message flows.
'   usecase:      Teach pool, message flow (dashed, crosses boundary), black-box vs expanded.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       business-process
' ============================================================================
title Example BPMN Collaboration - Buyer / Seller

' BPMN collaboration = multiple pools exchanging messages.
' PlantUML shape mapping:
'   rectangle (large)  = pool (participant)
'   rectangle (in pool)= task
'   circle (thin/double/thick) = start/intermediate/end event
'   usecase diamond    = gateway
'   -->                = sequence flow (within a pool)
'   ..> (dashed)       = message flow (crosses pool boundary)

' --- Pools (participants) -------------------------------------------------
' Expanded pool: shows internal process. Black-box pool: empty, only
' boundary visible — used when you don't model that participant's internals.

rectangle "**Buyer**" as PoolBuyer {
  usecase " " as BStart
  rectangle "Place Order" as B1
  rectangle "Receive Goods" as B2
  usecase " " as BEnd
  BStart --> B1
  B1 --> B2
  B2 --> BEnd
}

rectangle "**Seller**" as PoolSeller {
  usecase " " as SStart
  rectangle "Accept Order" as S1
  rectangle "Ship Goods" as S2
  usecase " " as SEnd
  SStart --> S1
  S1 --> S2
  S2 --> SEnd
}

' --- Message flows (dashed, cross pool boundary) -------------------------
B1 ..> S1 : "Order"
S2 ..> B2 : "Goods + Invoice"

note top of PoolBuyer
  <b>Expanded pool</b>
  Internal process visible.
  Tasks + sequence flow shown.
end note

note top of PoolSeller
  <b>Expanded pool</b>
  Contrast: a black-box pool
  would be empty with only
  message flows touching it.
end note

note right of PoolSeller
  <b>Message flow (dashed ..>)</b>
  Crosses the pool boundary.
  Sequence flow never crosses pools.
end note

@enduml
